---
import { Base, MinisiteNav } from '@oimlsmart/site-shell'
import '../styles/app.css'

const NAV = [
  { label: 'About', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Story', href: '/story' },
  { label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' },
  { label: 'Demo', href: '/demo' },
]
---

<Base title="The story" description="From the PDF Recommendation to the SMART one.">
  <MinisiteNav sections={NAV} base="/recs" />
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 docs-body">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-widest uppercase text-ink-muted mb-3">SMART Recommendations · Story</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold text-ink mb-6">From the PDF Recommendation to the SMART one</h1>

    <p>The OIML Recommendation has been a PDF for sixty years: the law of legal metrology, written for humans to read and laboratories to interpret by hand. Every test laboratory translates the same clauses into its own worksheets, and every translation drifts. The Recommendation says what the instrument must do; nothing in the PDF can check that anyone read it the same way.</p>

    <p>The Digital Calibration Certificate (DCC) work at PTB answered the certificate side of this: a certificate as structured, signed data rather than a scanned form. The SMART Recommendation is the same move one level earlier in the chain. If the certificate is data, the Recommendation it certifies against should be data too — the requirements, the test procedures, and the report forms, modeled so that a machine can evaluate them.</p>

    <h2>What the model keeps from the document</h2>
    <p>A SMART Recommendation is not a replacement document. The prose stands; every modeled element carries its clause URN back to the publication, so a reviewer can walk any requirement, test, or form field to its sentence in the source. The model is the same law, machine-readable — the document remains the authority of meaning, the model is its executable expression.</p>

    <h2>The corpus today</h2>
    <p>Four Recommendations carry the full model today: R 60 for load cells, R 91 for radar speed meters, R 129 for multi-dimensional measuring instruments, and R 144 for continuous gas analyzers. The OIML Certification System itself — the B 18 framework and its PD documents — is modeled the same way, so the whole path from application to certificate runs on modeled content.</p>

    <p class="mt-8"><a href="/recs/docs/" class="text-accent font-medium">The authoring documentation walks the method chapter by chapter →</a></p>
  </main>
</Base>
